 that, by the time of an Oliver Mtukudzi interview with Forbes Africa in 2016, it was '67 albums later' in his career.\" Is \"by the time\" an inference? The phrase \"67 albums later... telling Forbes Africa in 2016\" strongly indicates at that point. But \"67 albums later\" can be count by article time? Let's parse: \"67 albums later, Mtukudzi still spoke as if he was in search of what to call a career, telling Forbes Africa in 2016...\" This means at the point after 67 albums, he told Forbes Africa in 2016. So yes.\n\n2. The article says Forbes Africa interview occurred in 2016 (exact year only). C4 already says interview with Forbes Africa in 2016. Not new? Existing C4 says \"The same retrospective quotes an Oliver Mtukudzi interview with Forbes Africa in 2016.\" So no.\n\n3. No month. We cannot claim exact month.\n\nCould finding include quote: \"In the 2016 Forbes Africa interview, Mtukudzi said, 'I am yet to decide on a career to take on, because this is not a career for me. I am just doing me.'\" This is new factual content: the quote. It advances OneGap? It is from the feature in 2010s. But OneGap focuses exact date/month and album count. The quote doesn't directly address date/month or album count, except confirms 2016 feature. It adds a fact not represented. But \"materially advance this OneGap\" maybe somewhat: establishes content of feature. But the OneGap is about date/month and albums, not content. So probably not.\n\nMaybe finding 1 only. But need max 3. Could we extract \"The article says Mtukudzi's 2016 Forbes Africa interview occurred after '67 albums'\"? That's essentially same.\n\nNeed ensure evidence_refs enum W3.\n\nLet's think carefully. The prompt says \"Read the new observations in light of current OneGap and existing C. The OneGap tells you what matters, not what is true. Existing C helps avoid duplicates. Extract 0–3 NEW factual findings across this acquisition only when the observations directly support the full statement, materially advance this OneGap, and add facts not already adequately represented in C. If none, return findings=[].\"\n\nObservation has article. OneGap: \"The exact date/month of the Forbes Africa feature in the 2010s and the number of albums Mtukudzi had by that feature are not established.\"\n\nExisting C:\nC3: retrospective says \"67 albums later\" when discussing career.\nC4: same retrospective quotes interview with Forbes Africa in 2016.\n\nWhat does observation add for OneGap?\n- It confirms Forbes Africa interview in 2016 (not month). C4 already.\n- It links \"67 albums later\" and Forbes Africa 2016 in same sentence. C3 and C4 separate. Does that establish number of albums by that feature? Maybe. The number of albums by feature: 67. The article phrase \"67 albums later... telling Forbes Africa in 2016\" can be new linkage. This materially advances OneGap by associating 67 albums with the 2016 feature. It adds link not adequately represented in C. So finding 1.\n\nCould we also extract \"The article does not provide the exact month of the Forbes Africa feature, only 2016\"? That is a missing info label, not factual finding. No.\n\nCould extract \"The Forbes Africa interview occurred in 2016, according to the article.\" But C4 already says exactly. No new. Unless C4 says \"The same retrospective quotes an Oliver Mtukudzi interview with Forbes Africa in 2016.\" That is essentially same. So avoid duplicate.\n\nWhat about \"The article says Mtukudzi had 67 albums when he spoke to Forbes Africa in 2016.\" That is clearer and new link. Evidence W3. Good.\n\nIs there another new fact? \"Mtukudzi's first recording deal was in 1975\" not relevant. \"He joined Wagon Wheel band in 1977 alongside Thomas Mapfumo\" not relevant. \"Born September 1952\" not relevant to OneGap. \"Pakare Paye located in Norton about 45 km from Harare\" not relevant. \"Son Sam died in 2010 road accident\" etc. Not relevant.\n\nCould OneGap \"exact date/month of Forbes Africa feature in 2010s\" - observation